<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<graphml xmlns="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns http://graphml.graphdrawing.org/xmlns/1.0/graphml.xsd">
  <key id="d11" for="edge" attr.name="type" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d10" for="edge" attr.name="seq" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d9" for="edge" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d8" for="edge" attr.name="flow_id" attr.type="long" />
  <key id="d7" for="node" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d6" for="node" attr.name="service" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d5" for="node" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d4" for="graph" attr.name="graph_usable" attr.type="boolean" />
  <key id="d3" for="graph" attr.name="categories" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d2" for="graph" attr.name="notes" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d1" for="graph" attr.name="link" attr.type="string" />
  <key id="d0" for="graph" attr.name="name" attr.type="string" />
  <graph edgedefault="directed">
    <node id="0">
      <data key="d5">UserCompanyDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d6">UserCompanyDeveloper</data>
      <data key="d7">End user of the internal developer platform. Opens the Orion portal and requests a new environment, providing the environment name, business use case and business vertical.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1">
      <data key="d5">Route53</data>
      <data key="d6">Route53</data>
      <data key="d7">DNS resolution for the Orion portal; resolves to the Application Load Balancer.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2">
      <data key="d5">ALB</data>
      <data key="d6">ALB</data>
      <data key="d7">Application Load Balancer that sends the request to the Kubernetes pods.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3">
      <data key="d5">EKS</data>
      <data key="d6">EKS</data>
      <data key="d7">Container orchestrator running all workloads on 100% spot instances for cost efficiency. The main application pod creates one Kubernetes job per microservice (a business vertical may need 70+ microservices). AWS Node Termination Handler listens for spot termination events and drains nodes so workloads shift to another node. Scaled from 100 pods to 10,000+ pods. EKS replaces jobs disrupted by spot termination.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="4">
      <data key="d5">SNS</data>
      <data key="d6">SNS</data>
      <data key="d7">Receives an event whenever a job is triggered to create a microservice and fans the message out to SQS.</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5">
      <data key="d5">SQS</data>
      <data key="d6">SQS</data>
      <data key="d7">Holds job state messages. Replacement jobs watch the queue to detect unfinished jobs (e.g. a microservice launch that failed) and complete them, keeping a consistent state of environment provisioning and maintenance.</data>
    </node>
    <edge source="0" target="1" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">User opens the Orion portal; DNS resolution goes to Amazon Route 53.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1" target="2" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">Route 53 resolves the DNS to the Application Load Balancer.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="2" target="3" id="0">
      <data key="d8">0</data>
      <data key="d9">ALB sends the environment-creation request (name, business use case, business vertical) to the application pod running on EKS on spot instances.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">data</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="4" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">Whenever a Kubernetes job is triggered to create a microservice, it sends an event to SNS to store its state.</data>
      <data key="d10">0</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="3" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">The replacement job watches SQS for pending or unfinished jobs (e.g. disrupted by spot termination) and finishes launching the microservice.</data>
      <data key="d10">2</data>
      <data key="d11">meta</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="4" target="5" id="0">
      <data key="d8">1</data>
      <data key="d9">SNS fans out the message to SQS.</data>
      <data key="d10">1</data>
      <data key="d11">control</data>
    </edge>
    <data key="d0">OLX Autos: Internal Developer Platform (Orion) on Amazon EKS with 100% Spot Instances</data>
    <data key="d1">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1aYoIZvabbk</data>
    <data key="d2">OLX Autos operates in 11 countries and built an internal developer platform (Orion portal) to create replicas of its environments, reducing time to market and increasing deployment velocity. Workloads run on Amazon EKS with 100% spot instances. Kubernetes jobs launch microservices and persist their state through SNS to SQS, so replacement jobs can resume unfinished work after spot terminations.</data>
    <data key="d3">Containers, Developer Platform, Cost Optimization</data>
    <data key="d4">True</data>
  </graph>
</graphml>
